we're starting to think that someone  switched coffee brands over at riots  because they have gone into overdrive  their tactical FPS valor ant is  currently in closed beta and they've got  at least eight other projects on the go  including a fighting game an RPG and  even an animated series next on it's  jam-packed slate however is legends of  rune terror a collectible card game set  in the League of Legends universe call  another CCG we hear you sigh but sigh  not cynic because riot feels your pain  being punished for bad hands booster  packs huge gulfs between spendthrifts  and free to players not on their watch  here's what makes legends of ruin terror  like know of a CCG you played the most  fundamental change the CCG dynamic lies  at the heart of legends of runes harris  combat each game opens with one player  randomly assigned to the attacker role  for the other as defender attackers get  a play order token which means that they  have priority but and this is the big  difference the defender can then  immediately attempt to counter whatever  the attacker has thrown at them and  vice-versa  it's only when both players pass their  turns or run out of mana that the round  is considered complete and the attacker  and defender switch roles naturally  there are exceptions to this dynamic  evan flow for example spells come in  three speed types slow fast and burst  the latter can be deployed with  immediate effect without ending your  turn enabling you to effectively perform  two actions in one  if that sounds a little daunting  the Oracles eye is here to save you from  playing 40 chess this handy feature  effectively gives you a glimpse into the  future showing you the state of the  board after a sequence is played out and  removing any guesswork from the equation  legends of runeterra isn't altogether  passier and more aggressive CCG than you  might be used to case in point  summoning sickness that is the inability  to use a freshly summoned character to  attack or defend does not exist in this  dojo little wrinkles like this challenge  the CCG status quo without ever feeling  gratuitous another example that  illustrates this thoughtful  disruptiveness is its approach to mana  typically the classic system can feel  punitive if you're dealt a bad hand but  here you're given the opportunity to  funnel a maximum of three unspent mana  into a reserve that can then be used on  spells in the next round it's all serves  to put the emphasis on strategy  overlooked and leaves players feeling  that the balance of power could shift at  any given moment  as a league of Legends offshoot  runeterra has a deep reservoir of  characters to draw from so far  24 champions have made the cut that's  four in each of the games six regions  Andreas has cleverly adapted their  skills in the MOBA domain to operate  suitably yes authentically in the CCG  realm champions are more powerful than a  standard follower but they're certainly  not invulnerable and you can guarantee  that once they're in play your opponent  is going to focus their efforts on  taking them out as quickly as possible  however they are also able to level up  sometimes more than once if certain  conditions are met which grants them  game-changing abilities for example  looks in her standard form can summon  barriers to nullify damage against the  chosen Ally in play but once your rival  spends six or more manner on spells she  will level up and trigger her iconic  final spark which deals four damage at  zero mana cost  [Music]  it is however impossible to have  duplicates of the same champion in play  so while you can have duplicates in your  deck up to a maximum of three once one  card is in play the other two will  transform into spells again those spells  are analogues of the champions moves in  lol in Luxy standard form  it's prismatic barrier for example and  what you get depends on whether or not  that champion has leveled up of course  with nearly 150 champions to pick from 